## Correction to the conclusion — apply before running

What was established is "no reset observed while a debug session was
present", NOT "the rig cannot show the reaction". Three families are
alive and the runs so far do not separate them:

- F1  SBC is not evaluating / not configured to reset. The driver's
      variables say FWD is enabled; the SBC's own registers were never
      read. Candidates: FWD not enabled at silicon level, SBC not in the
      state where watchdogs run (INIT/SLEEP vs NORMAL), error-counter
      threshold never reached, or threshold configured to safe-state
      outputs only with no reset.
- F2  SBC reacts, reset does not reach the core. Two sub-cases:
      (a) OCDS session suppresses reset propagation — the SAME mechanism
          as H8 in the reset-type campaign (UDS hardReset, session
          present, no reboot). Two campaigns now show this shape.
      (b) board/HIL wiring: SBC reset output not connected to the MCU
          reset input on this rig, or a bench override holding it.
- F3  The wrong/withheld response never reaches the SPI (driver retry or
      timing masks it). Least likely after full withholding; kill it with
      the error-counter read in step 3, not by assumption.

Every step below must say which family it kills.

## This slot, in order

1. DESK CHECK (no rig time). From SbcDriver.c and the SBC datasheet
   write down, with register and bit names:
   a. which bit enables the FWD, which register holds the FWD error
      counter, what threshold triggers the reaction, and WHAT the
      configured reaction is on this build (reset to INIT vs safe-state
      outputs only);
   b. which SBC device state the watchdogs run in, and every condition
      under which the SBC stops or ignores them;
   c. every variable where the driver stores a READBACK of SBC status /
      config registers (device state, WD failure flags, error counter) —
      these are what step 3 reads. If the driver never reads back, say so:
      that alone is a finding (software belief with no silicon witness).
   d. from the board schematic / HIL wiring: which MCU reset input the
      SBC reset output drives, and whether the HIL bench has any jumper,
      relay, or DIO that overrides it. Also whether the SBC safe-state
      outputs are observable on the bench (DIO, scope, DMM).
   Output: a table "belief vs witness" — each F1/F2 claim with the
   observable that would decide it.

2. ONE NON-RESETTING ATTACH, READS ONLY — silicon truth on master.
   Read the readback variables from 1c: SBC device state, FWD enable,
   error counter, failure flags. Baseline while healthy.
   - Enable not set, or state not NORMAL -> F1 confirmed; record values,
     STOP, report. Do not proceed to injection.

3. ERROR-COUNTER PROBE (session still open, reads only). Trigger the
   withheld response via --fwd-selftest for ONE window period only, then
   poll the error counter / failure flags from 1c.
   - Counter does not move -> SBC not evaluating (F1) or response still
     arriving (F3): dump the SPI TX buffer contents for that window to
     tell them apart. STOP, report.
   - Counter increments and reaches threshold, no reset -> F1 (safe-state
     only) or F2. Continue.
   Detach cleanly, leave DUT in Standby.

4. SESSIONLESS REACTION TEST — the discriminator that has never run.
   TRACE32 CLOSED entirely (not NoDebug, no session). Rig idle, master
   build. Trigger the withheld response via --fwd-selftest. First confirm
   --fwd-selftest can be triggered with NO debug session; if it needs
   TRACE32, say so and instead use step 5's inject-then-detach procedure
   here. Witness WITHOUT the debugger: CAN silence / alive-counter
   restart / uptime rollover, UDS default-session drop, and the SBC
   safe-state outputs on the bench if 1d found them observable. Wait at
   most 60 s (a real threshold reaction is a few window periods, not
   minutes).
   Then attach fresh, non-resetting, read SCU_RSTSTAT and the stored
   reset-reason copy.
   - Reset observed -> F2(a) CONFIRMED: the debug session suppresses the
     reaction, on this test AND on the reset-type campaign's H8. Write
     that up as one finding. The PFM test procedure must close the
     session during the reaction window from now on.
   - No reset, safe-state outputs DID toggle -> F2(b) wiring/override:
     record what toggled, STOP, escalate with the schematic reference
     from 1d.
   - No reset, safe-state outputs did NOT toggle -> F1 (SBC never
     reacts): report with the step-2/3 register values. STOP.

5. FORMAL RUN, only if step 4 produced a reset. Reflash the PR build.
   S1 procedure: bring DUT to Drive, contactors closed, attach without
   reset, read healthy baseline, write cpBeginPending=1, confirm the latch
   (bit, healthy false, signature) — then CLOSE THE SESSION IMMEDIATELY
   and let the bench witness the reaction window (contactors open at
   ~5.5 s, reset expected shortly after). Re-attach non-resetting after
   the reboot: SCU_RSTSTAT changed, PFM healthy again.
   S1 passes -> run S2 (rewind refTimestampMs) with the same
   inject-then-detach procedure. Evidence CSV, before/after registers,
   exact commands, session-presence state recorded per step.

## Safety rails

- One non-resetting attach per step; on failure run the probe-health
  check and STOP.
- Never write SBC registers or config from the debugger; never change
  the SBC driver's configuration to "make it reset".
- Recovery from any stuck state is a POWER CYCLE, not a hand-clear.
- Leave the DUT in Standby and the probe fully closed at the end.
- Anything unexpected: stop, record, report — do not improvise.

